<Project>

  <!--
    Derived versioning for the Lz.* packages. Replaces LzVersion.props, which held the literal
    <LzVersion>0.12.0</LzVersion>; the version now comes from version.json plus the number of
    commits since that file last changed.

    WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE, imported by the five producers, rather than a line in
    CommonPackageHandling.targets. That file is imported by these five, by the packaging test
    fixture - which writes a scratch project whose SolutionDir points at this repo - and by the
    Schema project template. Putting the tool there would override the fixture's static version
    from inside a target, which is precisely the silent-override the eviction tests exist to catch,
    and would version a shipped template out of Lz's git history.

    (An earlier version of this comment said "NINE projects ... four ProjectTemplates". That
    overcounted. All four template imports carry Condition="!Exists('<own file>.csproj')", which is
    FALSE when the template is built in place, so three of them never import this file inside
    Lz.slnx at all. Schema.csproj is the exception only because its guard names ClientSDK.csproj -
    a copy-paste - so its condition is true in its own directory. Schema is IsPackable=false and
    produces no package either way. The placement conclusion is unchanged; the count was not.)

    GitVersionBaseDirectory pins the height to the repo root so all five compute ONE version. Do not
    reach for per-project pathFilters: pack asks each referenced project for its own PackageVersion,
    so a commit touching only Lz.Core would move Lz.Cli's nuspec DEPENDENCY while leaving Lz.Cli's
    own version alone, and one version would then name two different packages (NbgvSpike.md 4).

    Version is pinned exactly rather than floated: it is an input to every package this repo
    publishes, and to the lz runner that resolves Lz.Cli.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GitVersionBaseDirectory>$(SolutionDir)</GitVersionBaseDirectory>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Nerdbank.GitVersioning" Version="3.10.94" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A SHALLOW clone is loud - NBGV throws MSB4018 rather than guessing. NO git repository at all is
    NOT: measured against 3.10.94, a source tree with version.json and no .git builds successfully,
    with no warning, at "0.12.0-g" - height 0, and a prerelease that sorts strictly BELOW the
    published 0.12.0. That is the one state this scheme can silently regress in, so refuse it.

    Reachable rather than theoretical: a source archive instead of a clone, or a container build
    that copies the tree without .git - which MigrationPlan section 7 already flags as a cost of
    derived versioning.

    Exists() is true for a worktree's .git FILE as well as a normal directory, so this does not
    fire on a linked worktree.
  -->
  <Target Name="RefuseAVersionWithNoGitHistory"
          BeforeTargets="GetBuildVersion;Build"
          Condition="!Exists('$(GitVersionBaseDirectory).git')">
    <Error Text="No git repository at $(GitVersionBaseDirectory), so the version cannot be derived. Nerdbank.GitVersioning does not fail here - it would quietly produce 0.12.0-g at height 0, a PRERELEASE that sorts below the released 0.12.0, and every package this build produced would carry it. Build from a clone (a full one: a shallow clone fails separately, in NBGV itself)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
